---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on Coinbase (TOTP Authenticator)';

const description =
  'Secure your Coinbase account with two-factor authentication codes generated in your browser. Get the TOTP setup key from Coinbase security settings.';

---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="Coinbase"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/coinbase/"
  dateModified="2026-11-09"
  datePublished="2026-11-09"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-23"
  dateModified="2026-11-23"
  sources={[
    {
      label: 'Coinbase Help — Set up your 2-step verification',
      href: 'https://help.coinbase.com/en/coinbase/getting-started/getting-started-with-coinbase/2-step-verification',
      kind: 'official',
    },
    {
      label: 'RFC 6238 — TOTP: Time-Based One-Time Password Algorithm',
      href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6238',
      kind: 'independent',
    },
  ]}
  intro="Coinbase protects an exchange balance, so 2FA here is the difference between an inconvenience and a headline. It supports standard TOTP with any authenticator, including a browser-based one — though Coinbase’s setup flow expects an authenticator app on a mobile device, so plan for the phone to be in the loop once. The platform pairs 2FA with a few crypto-specific gates worth setting up the same day."
  minutes={3}
  params={['6 digits', '30s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="On Coinbase, safeguarding the secret is safeguarding the funds"
  ctaBody="SMS 2FA on a crypto account is a standing SIM-swap invitation — the app-based enrollment above is the fast fix. The Base32 key and the withdrawal-whitelist screenshot belong in your encrypted vault the moment the dialog closes."
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open Coinbase security settings',
      body: 'Sign in at coinbase.com and open the 2-step verification settings page — usually under the avatar menu, then Security. Coinbase’s help centre calls this page “2-step verification settings” throughout, and lists every method you can enable there.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Add an authenticator method',
      body: 'Under “Available methods”, click “Set up” next to “Authenticator app” rather than choosing SMS. Coinbase first confirms it is you — a password prompt or an existing 2FA check, depending on the account.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Extract the setup key',
      body: 'Coinbase shows a QR code during setup and its help centre says the flow “requires accessing the authenticator app from your mobile device” — so the phone is usually in the loop even though the settings live on the web. Screenshot the QR and paste the image into the generator to decode it locally; if your build also reveals a secret key, copying that is equivalent.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate and confirm',
      body: 'Copy the live 6-digit code and enter it in Coinbase’s verification field. Coinbase marks the TOTP method as active and shows it alongside any passkeys or hardware keys you keep.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Pull the key into your vault',
      body: 'Crypto accounts are high-value targets: store the Base32 secret in an encrypted password manager immediately, and double-check the withdrawal-address whitelist that Coinbase offers so a stolen session alone can’t redirect funds.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Coinbase Authenticator app vs. SMS — which should I pick?',
      a: 'Always the authenticator app. Coinbase requires 2-step verification on every account and its help centre calls SMS “the least secure option” itself. On a crypto exchange that matters more than elsewhere: an attacker who ports your number receives every SMS code, while TOTP tokens never transit a phone network.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I removed or replaced my 2FA — why can’t I send crypto out?',
      a: 'Coinbase has used a short security hold on outbound sends after authentication changes. Where one applies it gives the real owner time to undo an attacker’s edits, so treat a temporary send hold as expected rather than as fresh evidence of compromise — but check the current policy when you rotate this key, because the window is set by Coinbase, not by the standard.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does this replace the Coinbase mobile app’s prompts?',
      a: 'It replaces code generation, not the policy. Coinbase may still send push notifications about sign-ins from new devices and ask to confirm unusual activity regardless of the second factor you use.',
    },
    {
      q: 'I lost my Coinbase TOTP key — how do I get back in?',
      a: 'Without the secret you’ll need Coinbase’s recovery path — identity verification, or the trusted-contacts feature Coinbase documents, where two to five people you nominate approve a recovery request (usable 24 hours after setup). Either route is slow, which is the point of step 5: the Base32 key in an encrypted vault is your own backup, and it can re-mint codes anywhere, forever.',
    },
  ]}
/>
